Hacking on foxtrot
==================

Coding style
------------

ISO C99, enforced with -std=c99 -pedantic.  Do not use C11 features
(_Static_assert, <stdatomic.h>, _Alignas, anonymous unions, _Generic);
if one is wanted, wrap it in a macro with a C99 fallback.  Follow the GNU
Coding Standards: GNU indentation, a space before the opening parenthesis
of calls and declarations, return type on its own line in definitions, and
no tabs beyond what GNU indent produces.  Every public symbol starts with
gf_ (types and functions) or GF_ (macros, enumerators).  Symbols shared
between source files but not public start with gfi_.  The library exports
nothing but gf_ symbols.

Every source file carries the GPLv3+ notice used in the existing files.

The library does no I/O
-----------------------

No sockets, no files, no threads, no signals, no stderr.  Bytes come in
through a receive call and go out through a write callback; time is passed
in by the caller.  Anything that needs the outside world belongs in
integration/ or in the application.

Push-parsing contract (required for every codec that reads a stream)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

  * Input arrives in chunks.  The parser never blocks and never reads
    from a file descriptor.
  * Stream parsers take (buf, len), consume what they can, and advance
    both.  Whatever is left is an incomplete unit that the caller keeps
    and presents again with more data appended.  GF_NEED_MORE reports that
    case; it is not an error.
  * Lengths are checked as soon as they are readable, before waiting for
    the bytes they announce.
  * Nothing authenticated is delivered before its tag has been verified.
  * Output is a stream of semantic events to a handler.  Variable-length
    payloads are slices into the caller's buffer.
  * State machines are explicit (an enum or flags plus small scratch
    fields), not hidden in the call stack.
  * Unit tests must feed the same input whole and in every chunk size from
    1 upwards and compare the results.  See tests/test-packet.c.

Messages inside an authenticated packet are parsed from a complete buffer
with gf_reader (wire.h): all-or-nothing reads, no allocation, and a
message with bytes left over after its last field is malformed.

Security policy
---------------

The policy (include/foxtrot/policy.h) is closed.  Do not add a
configuration option that enables something the manual says is absent.
A new algorithm needs a known-answer test from its defining RFC or draft,
or from an independent implementation (the tests/gen-*-vectors.py scripts
use python's cryptography package and hashlib), and an entry in the
"Security policy" chapter of the manual.

Every check that exists to refuse something needs a test that fails when
the check is removed.  Try it: delete the check, run "make check", and
see that something breaks.  tests/test-conn-attack.c has the pattern.

Cryptography
------------

Never implement a primitive.  Everything comes from libgcrypt through
src/crypto.c, src/packet.c, src/kex.c and src/key.c.  libgcrypt's ECDH
does not validate the peer's point: use gfi_ec_point_ok first.  Wipe key
material with gf_wipe.  Compare secrets with gf_ct_equal.

Warnings
--------

The tree must build with zero compiler warnings (configure adds -Wall
-Wextra and more).  Fix the cause; if a warning is genuinely unavoidable,
add a narrow, commented suppression.  Read the build output, not just the
exit status.

Testing
-------

  make check              # unit tests
  make check-integration  # integration tests
  make distcheck          # before every release

make check runs unit tests only (tests/): deterministic, in-process, with
no sockets, files, child processes or wall clock (connections take the
clock as an argument and the loopback tests use a virtual one).  Anything
that needs real I/O, another program or the host's own files goes in
integration/, which make check-integration builds and runs and make check
never touches:

  interop-openssh.sh  foxtrot's client against sshd and ssh against
                      foxtrot's server (interop-client and interop-server
                      are our side), through key exchange, public key
                      (also host-bound), password and keyboard-interactive
                      authentication, commands, bulk transfers with
                      rekeying, port forwarding and SFTP; needs ssh, sftp,
                      ssh-keygen and sshd (python3 for the forwarding
                      test).  Passwords and codes reach ssh through
                      SSH_ASKPASS, so no tty is needed.  It also runs
                      OpenSSH user and host certificates (ssh-keygen -s,
                      TrustedUserCAKeys, HostCertificate, @cert-authority)
                      and known_hosts both ways.
  interop-keyfiles.sh passphrase-protected private key files against
                      ssh-keygen, with the real bcrypt-pbkdf (interop-key
                      supplies it from python3's bcrypt module, as an
                      application would supply its own); needs ssh-keygen
                      and python3 with the bcrypt module.

It skips itself (exit 77) when what it needs is missing, and when
GF_NO_INTEROP is set.  Set SSHD if sshd is not in /usr/sbin.

OpenSSH refuses a rekey before authentication, so the rekeying tests
against it authenticate first.

The test vector headers (tests/vectors-*.h) are generated by the
tests/gen-*-vectors.py scripts and checked in; regenerate them with the
scripts, do not edit them.  vectors-key.h holds throwaway test keys.  The
generators need ssh-keygen and python3's "cryptography" package (and
"bcrypt", and the openssl command, for the key file and X.509 vectors);
nothing in make check does.  Where a vector comes from the format and not from
an implementation of it (the security keys, the RFC 6187 signatures), the
generator says so and checks it against one that can (ssh-keygen -Y, openssl
verify).

Copyright assignment
--------------------

The project intends to become part of the GNU system with copyright
assigned to the Free Software Foundation.  Until then, keep the notice
"Copyright (C) YEAR Name" in each file, and do not incorporate code whose
licence is not compatible with GPLv3+.

Documentation and translation
-----------------------------

The manual is Texinfo (doc/foxtrot.texi); update it with any change to
design, policy or roadmap.  Build it with "make info" or "make pdf".

Every string a person might read (for example the text returned by
gf_strerror) must be wrapped in _() from src/gettext.h and its source file
listed in po/POTFILES.in.  Text sent to a peer (disconnect descriptions) is
English and fixed, and is not translated.  Do not translate RFC-defined
identifiers.
